Example 2 Let a business firm produces three types of products \(\mathrm{P}_{1}, \mathrm{P}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{P}_{3}\) that uses three types of raw materials \(R_{1}, R_{2}\) and \(R_{3}\). Let the firm has purchase orders from two clients \(\mathrm{F}_{1}\) and \(\mathrm{F}_{2}\). Considering the situation that the firm has a limited quantity of \(R_{1}, R_{2}\) and \(R_{3}\), respectively, prepare a model to determine the quantities of the raw material \(R_{1}, R_{2}\) and \(R_{3}\) required to meet the purchase orders.
Solution Step 1 The physical situation is well identified in the problem.
Step 2 Let A be a matrix that represents purchase orders from the two clients \(\mathrm{F}_{1}\) and \(\mathrm{F}_{2}\). Then, A is of the form
\[\left.\mathrm{A}=\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathrm{P}_{1} & \mathrm{P}_{2} & \mathrm{P}_{3} \\
\mathrm{~F}_{1} \\
\mathrm{~F}_{2}[\bullet & \bullet & \bullet
\end{array}\right]\]

Let B be the matrix that represents the amount of raw materials \(\mathrm{R}_{1}, \mathrm{R}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{R}_{3}\), required to manufacture each unit of the products \(\mathrm{P}_{1}, \mathrm{P}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{P}_{3}\). Then, B is of the form
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/f750de5f-2c52-4290-b4df-5a275c636b7e-025.jpg?height=219&width=266&top_left_y=1900&top_left_x=679)

Step 3 Note that the product (which in this case is well defined) of matrices A and B is given by the following matrix
\[A B=\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{R}_{1} \mathrm{R}_{2} \mathrm{R}_{3} \\
\mathrm{~F}_{1}\left[\begin{array}{l}
\bullet \\
\mathrm{F}_{2}[\cdot \bullet \bullet \cdot \\
]
