\[\hat{n}=\frac{\vec{b}_{1} \times \vec{b}_{2}}{\left|\vec{b}_{1} \times \vec{b}_{2}\right|}\]

Then
\[\overrightarrow{\mathrm{PQ}}=d \hat{n}\]
where, \(d\) is the magnitude of the shortest distance vector. Let \(\theta\) be the angle between \(\overrightarrow{\mathrm{ST}}\) and \(\overrightarrow{\mathrm{PQ}}\). Then
\[\mathrm{PQ}=\mathrm{ST}|\cos \theta|\]

But
\[\begin{array}{rlr}
\cos \theta & =\left|\frac{\overrightarrow{\mathrm{PQ}} \cdot \overrightarrow{\mathrm{ST}}}{|\overrightarrow{\mathrm{PQ}}||\overrightarrow{\mathrm{ST}}|}\right| & \\
& =\left|\frac{d \hat{n} \cdot\left(\vec{a}_{2}-\vec{a}_{1}\right)}{d \mathrm{ST}}\right| \quad\left(\text { since } \overrightarrow{\mathrm{ST}}=\vec{a}_{2}-\vec{a}_{1}\right) \\
& =\left|\frac{\left(\vec{b}_{1} \times \vec{b}_{2}\right) \cdot\left(\vec{a}_{2}-\vec{a}_{1}\right)}{\mathrm{ST}\left|\vec{b}_{1} \times \vec{b}_{2}\right|}\right| & \text { [From (3)] }
\end{array}\]

Hence, the required shortest distance is
or
\[\begin{aligned}
